{"name":"Beauty and the Beach","hex":"#c99680","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"beauty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The quality of being (especially visually) attractive, pleasing, fine or good-looking; comeliness.","Someone who is beautiful."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Thanks!","Cool!"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beauty"},{"term":"and","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Ansus."]},{"part":"Conjunction","definitions":["As a coordinating conjunction; expressing two elements to be taken together or in addition to each other.","Yet; but."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/and"},{"term":"beach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The shore of a body of water, especially when sandy or pebbly.","A horizontal strip of land, usually sandy, adjoining water."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To run aground on a beach.","To run (something) aground on a beach."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beach"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beauty and the Beach","blocks":[{"text":"Beauty and the Beach is a 1941 American short musical film directed by Leslie M. Roush. It was nominated for an Academy Award at the 14th Academy Awards for Best Short Subject (One-Reel)."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beauty_and_the_Beach"},{"title":"Beauty","blocks":[{"text":"Beauty is commonly described as a feature of objects that makes them pleasurable to perceive. Such objects include landscapes, sunsets, humans and works of art. Beauty, art and taste are the main subjects of aesthetics, one of the fields of study within philosophy. As a positive aesthetic value, it is contrasted with ugliness as its negative counterpart."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg/330px-Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beauty"},{"title":"Beach","blocks":[{"text":"A beach is a landform alongside a body of water which consists of loose particles. The particles composing a beach are typically made from rock, such as sand, gravel, shingle, pebbles, etc., or biological sources, such as mollusc shells or coralline algae. Sediments settle in different densities and structures, depending on the local wave action and weather, creating different textures, colors and gradients or layers of material."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Beach_at_Fort_Lauderdale.jpg/330px-Beach_at_Fort_Lauderdale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beach"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg/500px-Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg/1280px-Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg/500px-Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Rayonnant style rose window in Notre-Dame de Paris. In Gothic architecture, light was considered \"the source and actual essence of all that is beautiful\".[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Analysis_of_Beauty_Plate_1_by_William_Hogarth.jpg/500px-Analysis_of_Beauty_Plate_1_by_William_Hogarth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Analysis_of_Beauty_Plate_1_by_William_Hogarth.jpg/1280px-Analysis_of_Beauty_Plate_1_by_William_Hogarth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Analysis_of_Beauty_Plate_1_by_William_Hogarth.jpg/500px-Analysis_of_Beauty_Plate_1_by_William_Hogarth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In The Analysis of Beauty, William Hogarth depicts the effect of changing proportions and attempts to define what proportions are beautiful.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnalysis_of_Beauty_Plate_1_by_William_Hogarth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Helene_Paris_Louvre_K6.jpg/500px-Helene_Paris_Louvre_K6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Helene_Paris_Louvre_K6.jpg/1280px-Helene_Paris_Louvre_K6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Helene_Paris_Louvre_K6.jpg/500px-Helene_Paris_Louvre_K6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Greek mythology mentions Helen of Troy (left) as the most beautiful woman.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHelene_Paris_Louvre_K6.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Sandro_Botticelli_-_La_nascita_di_Venere_-_Google_Art_Project_-_edited.jpg/500px-Sandro_Botticelli_-_La_nascita_di_Venere_-_Google_Art_Project_-_edited.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Sandro_Botticelli_-_La_nascita_di_Venere_-_Google_Art_Project_-_edited.jpg/1280px-Sandro_Botticelli_-_La_nascita_di_Venere_-_Google_Art_Project_-_edited.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb